{"maxId":92,"entries":{"chapter-106-study-urinary-tract-infections|Chapter 106 Study: Urinary Tract Infections":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"definition-amp-epidemiology|Definition & epidemiology":["u3"],"copy|ASB is bacteriuria, with or without pyuria, without localizing genitourinary symptoms; bacteriuria in older adults is usually asymptomatic colonization. (p. 1699)":["u4"],"copy|Simple cystitis stays within the bladder; complicated UTI extends beyond it and includes pyelonephritis. Recurrent UTI: ≥3 in 12 months or ≥2 in 6 months, after clinical resolution of each treated episode. (p. 1700)":["u5"],"diagnosis-amp-management|Diagnosis & management":["u6"],"copy|Do not screen/treat ASB routinely; the exception discussed for older adults is endoscopic urologic procedures associated with mucosal trauma. (p. 1703)":["u7"],"copy|Urine-character changes alone do not establish UTI. McGeer criteria serve surveillance; Loeb criteria guide antibiotic decisions. (p. 1705)":["u8"],"copy|With nonspecific symptoms but no UTI criteria or warning signs, use active monitoring, hydration and alternative-diagnosis assessment. Fever, rigors, unstable vitals or clear-cut delirium require evaluation. (p. 1712)":["u9"],"drugs-acute-simple-cystitis|Drugs — acute simple cystitis":["u10"],"copy|Nitrofurantoin monohydrate/macrocrystals 100 mg twice daily for 5 days; avoid with CrCl <30 mL/min or long-term suppression. (p. 1708)":["u11"],"copy|TMP/SMX 160/800 mg twice daily for 3 days; avoid empiric use when local uropathogen resistance exceeds 20%. (p. 1708)":["u12"],"copy|TMP/SMX interacts with warfarin and sulfonylureas; monitor increased INR/hypoglycemia risk. (p. 1709)":["u13"],"copy|Fosfomycin 3 g once orally: generally reserved for resistant pathogens or inability to use other first-line drugs; not for complicated UTI. (p. 1709)":["u14"],"copy|Most older men with simple cystitis: 7 days if response is prompt, within 72 hours; assess for extension beyond the bladder. (p. 1711)":["u15"],"exam-traps-amp-prevention|Exam traps & prevention":["u16"],"copy|Male sex alone does not make every UTI complicated. (p. 1710)":["u17"],"copy|Nitrofurantoin/fosfomycin lack adequate renal penetration for complicated UTI. (p. 1711)":["u18"],"copy|Avoid unnecessary catheters and remove promptly when no longer indicated; routine fixed-interval replacement is not recommended. (p. 1714)":["u19"],"recall|Recall":["u20"],"past-paper-questions-on-this-chapter|Past-paper questions on this chapter":["u21"],"copy|2023-Jun Q55 — mcq-526291ef04bcef123eab45db":["u22"],"copy|2024-May Q19 — mcq-81abf0ac32298245ce5a44eb":["u23"],"copy|2026-Jun Q84 — mcq-f7b82a0ad50cab63832650a2":["u24"],"copy|2025-Jun Q12 — mcq-60d358f39722f9df5a3990cc":["u25"],"table-106-2-book-image|Table 106-2 — book image":["u26"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 106-2, p.1703":["u27","u49"],"copy|CHAPTER 106s / HAZZARD":["u28"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 106; the book is the reference.":["u29"],"copy|Full chapter available: Read Chapter 106.":["u30"],"copy|Fast build from the book; not lane-reviewed.":["u31"],"copy|3.":["u32"],"copy|How is recurrent UTI defined?":["u33"],"copy|7.":["u34"],"copy|What are the different purposes of McGeer and Loeb criteria?":["u35"],"copy|8.":["u36"],"copy|What belongs in active monitoring for nonspecific symptoms?":["u37"],"copy|9.":["u38"],"copy|What nitrofurantoin regimen and renal limit does the chapter give for simple cystitis?":["u39"],"copy|10.":["u40"],"copy|What TMP/SMX regimen and local-resistance cutoff are given?":["u41"],"copy|11.":["u42"],"copy|Which important TMP/SMX interactions should be recognized?":["u43"],"copy|12.":["u44"],"copy|What is the fosfomycin dose and its usual place in simple-cystitis treatment?":["u45"],"copy|15.":["u46"],"copy|What warning signs prevent a nonspecific presentation from being treated as routine observation alone?":["u47"],"copy|2023-Jun Q55":["u22","u48"],"copy|Answer":["u50","u56","u58","u60","u66","u71","u73","u76"],"copy|At least":["u51"],"copy|3 symptomatic UTIs in 12 months":["u52"],"copy|or":["u53"],"copy|2 in 6 months":["u54"],"copy|, following clinical resolution of each episode with treatment. (p. 1700)":["u55"],"copy|Original/revised McGeer criteria support nursing-home surveillance; Loeb minimum criteria support clinical antibiotic decisions. (p. 1705)":["u57"],"copy|Frequent vital-sign, hydration and symptom checks; hydration support; alternative-diagnosis assessment; and explicit criteria for notifying the physician about deterioration or persistence. (p. 1705)":["u59"],"copy|Monohydrate/macrocrystals":["u61"],"copy|100 mg twice daily for 5 days":["u62"],"copy|; avoid if CrCl is":["u63"],"copy|<30 mL/min":["u64"],"copy|, and avoid long-term suppression. (p. 1708)":["u65"],"copy|160/800 mg twice daily for 3 days":["u67"],"copy|for simple cystitis; avoid empiric use if local uropathogen resistance exceeds":["u68"],"copy|20%":["u69"],"copy|. (p. 1708)":["u70"],"copy|Warfarin, with increased INR, and sulfonylureas, with increased hypoglycemia risk. (p. 1709)":["u72"],"copy|3 g as a single oral dose":["u74"],"copy|; generally reserved for highly resistant pathogens or inability to use other first-line agents. It is not recommended for complicated UTI. (p. 1709)":["u75"],"copy|Fever, rigors/chills, unstable vital signs or new clear-cut delirium warrant evaluation for infection and noninfectious causes; assess whether the presentation is consistent with UTI. (p. 1712)":["u77"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u21"],"copy|2024-May Q19":["u23"],"copy|2026-Jun Q84":["u24"],"copy|2025-Jun Q12":["u25"],"copy|CHAPTER 106 / HAZZARD":["u28"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[93,-15],[108,0]]}
